1
00:00:01,770 --> 00:00:07,650
We're told AI makes junior workers incredibly productive, but a new trial reveals it actually

2
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:12,450
makes them 17% worse at understanding the exact work they just finished.

3
00:00:12,830 --> 00:00:14,110
Meet our junior developer.

4
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:17,670
They're tasked with mastering a brand new programming library on the clock.

5
00:00:18,130 --> 00:00:22,210
Instead of struggling through the dense documentation, they just ask an AI to write the logic.

6
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:24,990
On their screen, the project is finished in seconds.

7
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:29,750
But inside the developer's head, the mental blueprint for how that code actually works

8
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:31,090
remains completely empty.

9
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:36,370
By outsourcing the frustration of fixing errors to a bot, the brain skips the exact friction

10
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,190
required to build permanent expertise.

11
00:00:38,770 --> 00:00:42,890
The real danger hits when the AI is taken away, and they have to fix a broken script

12
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:43,230
alone.

13
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:47,470
Researchers found these AI-assisted workers specifically bombed at debugging.

14
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:52,510
If we use AI to skip the struggle of learning today, we won't have the skills to supervise

15
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:53,310
its mistakes tomorrow.

